\description{
Classify an explicit prediction domain, decompose native link predictors,
and compare full-domain indices with restricted-domain and term-omission
predictions. No model is refitted.
}
\details{
The default sampling distance is a warned grid-spacing heuristic,
not inferred cell areas. Sampling interpretation depends on the supplied
cell size or distance. Reporting classes always
describe actual same-year support: \code{sampled}, \code{previously_sampled}
(sampled in another year, not necessarily an earlier year), and
\code{never_sampled}. The chosen definitions govern omission masks and
sampled-only domains.

Contributions add on the link scale, separately for delta components;
they must not be back-transformed and added on the response scale.
Decomposition quantiles use reference-weighted, 1024-bin histograms
spanning each group's observed range; their resolution is recorded in
metadata. Means and SDs use all positive-weight cells. Only compact
annual summaries and histograms are accumulated, unless cells are retained.

Omission is a prediction-target counterfactual, \strong{not a refit}; use
\code{\link[=plot_step]{plot_step()}} to study refitted models. Fields are set to zero; the year
term is replaced by its first-year value at the same reference row.
\code{"sampled_mean"} replaces a covariate's additive contribution by its
reference-weighted mean over cells sampled that year, separately in each
joint draw and delta component. It is not the prediction at a mean raw
covariate. \code{"zero"} is appropriate only for deliberately centred terms.
\code{"fixed"} reevaluates the term at explicit raw predictor values, retaining
its other predictors; this also handles interactions and smooths.

Weighted-mean restricted indices renormalise weights each year. An empty
restricted mean is undefined (\code{NA}); an empty restricted total is zero.
\code{"cell_time"} is a variable-domain diagnostic, not an alternative index.
Use this object's sampled-only plot for intentional comparisons of
different domain areas. Ordinary \code{\link[=index_ratio]{index_ratio()}} and \code{\link[=plot_compare]{plot_compare()}}
preserve their equal-area safeguards; do not change area metadata to
bypass them.

GAMs use blocked native linear-predictor matrices and jointly sampled
coefficients. Smoothing-parameter corrected covariance is used when
available; otherwise it is conditional on fitted smoothing parameters.
Likelihood nuisance parameters are held fitted. This is Gaussian
coefficient-simulation uncertainty, not new MCMC or the delta-method
approximation used by ordinary \code{\link[=cpue_index]{cpue_index()}} GAM summaries. Natural
single-response Gaussian, binomial, Poisson, Gamma, negative-binomial,
and Tweedie GAMs are supported; multi-predictor distributional families
and transformed responses fail explicitly. Formula offsets are retained.
Slopes use numeric year labels when available, otherwise ordered year
positions. All comparisons use paired joint Gaussian draws, not
independent marginal intervals. Nonpositive series have undefined log
comparisons; no small positive replacement is made.

Plotting and table extraction reuse stored results; they do not
predict again or refit a model. Sampled-only comparisons divide every
displayed series and its interval by the geometric mean over years with
finite positive estimates in every displayed series. Weighted means use
the full index's geometric mean for every series, preserving level
differences. Area totals use each series' own geometric mean over that
common period to compare shapes rather than different domain areas.
This display-only normalisation leaves stored results unchanged; the lower
panel shows coverage, not uncertainty in the sampling process. Unavailable
years remain gaps. Other index panels retain the stored index scale.
Decomposition ribbons show stored spatial contribution quantiles
(\code{Q10} to \code{Q90}), not parameter-uncertainty intervals.

Omission is a prediction counterfactual, not a refitted reduced model.
Contributions are additive on the link scale, not on the response scale.
A sampled-only domain can change between years and is not a fixed-domain
abundance index. Classification depends on the declared mapping and
sampling thresholds, not on interpolation or a mesh boundary. When no
sampling rule is supplied, the default half-grid-spacing distance rule
warns: it is a convenience convention, not an inferred survey footprint.
All three sampled-only definitions can be compared while omission uses
the single stored \code{omission_definition} (by default \code{"cell_time"}).
Year-effect omission uses the first year as its baseline. Covariate
omission defaults to the sampled-year mean link contribution as its
reference; this is not prediction at a mean response-scale covariate.
